<section xml:id="sec-ch05-04-GreensTheoremDivergence" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <title>Green's Theorem: 2D Divergence</title>

  <note>
    <p>
      Suppose you have a SCPS curve <m>C</m> representing a boundary
      enclosing a region <m>R</m> inside some liquid whose particles motion
      is given by a vector field F. As before, it makes sense that the
      behaviour of the liquid particles in <m>R</m> determines the behaviour
      of the particles near the boundary. For example, if for all (most)
      points in <m>R</m>, particles near them are moving outwards (away
      from the point), it makes sense that the same is happening near the
      boundary (particles are moving outwards across the boundary <m>C</m>
      ), as shown below:
    </p>
  </note>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch05-04-outward-motion">
    <caption>A simple closed smooth curve, traversed counter-clockwise, with vectors placed on it, pointing outwards perpendicular to its tangent vector. The curve is enclosing multiple points from which vectors points away from in all cardinal directions.</caption>

    <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_11_25_8a4355876d2d2a66647eg-1. -->
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch05-04-outward-motion" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
      <instructions>
        <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
        <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
      </instructions>
      <static>
        <image source="/images/2025_11_25_8a4355876d2d2a66647eg-1.png">
          <shortdescription>A simple closed smooth curve, traversed counter-clockwise, with vectors placed on it, pointing outwards perpendicular to its tangent vector. The curve is enclosing multiple points from which vectors points away from in all cardinal directions.</shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>A simple closed smooth curve, traversed counter-clockwise, with vectors placed on it, pointing outwards perpendicular to its tangent vector. The curve is enclosing multiple points from which vectors points away from in all cardinal directions.</p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <p>
    We quantify the expansion (outwards/repelling motion) of particles at
    a point using divergence.
  </p>

  <definition>
    <title>2D Divergence</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\vec{F}=\langle f(x, y), g(x, y)\rangle</m> be a
        differentiable vector field representing the motion of particles.
        We measure the outwards/repelling motion at a point ( <m>a, b</m> )
        by using
        <md>
          \operatorname{Div} \vec{F}(a, b)=\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}(a, b)+\frac{\partial g}{\partial y}(a, b)
        </md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <note>
    <p>
      When we say <q>at a point</q>, we really mean inside an infinitely
      small circle centered at <m>(a, b)</m>.
    </p>
  </note>

  <remark>
    <title>The sign of <m>\operatorname{Div} \vec{F}(a, b)</m></title>

    <p>
      The sign of <m>\operatorname{Div} \vec{F}(a, b)</m> tells us whether
      particles are expanding or contracting at a point:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            If <m>\operatorname{Div} \vec{F}(a, b) \gt 0,(a, b)</m> is acting
            as a repellent (particles are moving away from <m>(a, b)</m>).
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            If <m>\operatorname{Div} \vec{F}(a, b) \lt 0,(a, b)</m> is acting
            as an attractor (particles are moving towards <m>(a, b)</m> ).
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            If <m>\operatorname{Div} \vec{F}(a, b)=0</m>, there is no
            discernible repellent/attractor motion around <m>(a, b)</m> and
            we say <m>(a, b)</m> is not a source.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch05-04-ex-26-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Given the vector field
        <m>\vec{F}(x, y)=\left\langle x^2+y^2+x, y^{2}+x^2+y\right\rangle</m> :
      </p>

      <p>(a) Compute Div F.</p>

      <p>
        (b) Find a point that acts as an attractor and a point that acts as
        a repellent.
      </p>

      <p>(c) Find a point that is not a source.</p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        <md>
          \operatorname{Div}\vec F = f_x + g_y = 2x+1 +2y +1 = 2x+2y+2
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Attractor implies <m>\operatorname{Div} \vec F \lt 0 </m> and
        repellent <m>\operatorname{Div} \vec F \gt 0</m>
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\operatorname{Div}\vec F \lt 0 \Rightarrow 2x+2y+2 \lt 0 \Rightarrow y \lt -1-x</mrow>
          <mrow>\text{An easy point for this purpose, satisfying the above restriction is }(0,-2), \ \operatorname{Div}\vec F(0,-2) = -2</mrow>
          <mrow>\operatorname{Div}\vec F \gt 0 \Rightarrow 2x+2y+2 \gt 0 \Rightarrow y \gt -1-x</mrow>
          <mrow>\text{An easy point for this purpose, satisfying the above restriction is }(0,0), \ \operatorname{Div}\vec F(0,0) = 2</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Not a source implies zero Div.
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\operatorname{Div} \vec F = 0 \Rightarrow 2x+2y+2 = 0 \Rightarrow y = -1-x</mrow>
          <mrow>\text{An easy point for this purpose, satisfying the above restriction is }(0,-1), \ \operatorname{Div}\vec F(0,-1) = 0</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <p>
    This relationship between expansion inside a region and its boundary
    is quantified mathematically by Green's Theorem.
  </p>

  <theorem>
    <title>Green's Theorem: Divergence</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>C</m> be a SCPS curve, oriented counterclockwise, that
        encloses a connected and simply connected region <m>R</m> in the
        plane. Assume that <m>\vec{F}=\langle f, g\rangle</m>, where
        <m>f</m> and <m>g</m> have continuous first partial derivatives in
        <m>R</m>. Then
        <md>
          \oint_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{N} d s=\iint_{R}\left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial x}+\frac{\partial g}{\partial y}\right) d A
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <remark>
    <p>
      Given <m>\vec{F}=\langle f(x, y), g(x, y)\rangle</m> and
      <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle x(t), y(t)\rangle</m>, the following are
      equivalent:
      <md>
        \int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{N} d s=\int_{C} f d y-g d x
      </md>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <note>
    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Since we are dealing with curl, we assume that <m>C</m> is
            simple and closed (so that it encloses a region <m>R</m> ).
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Important: This theorem says that in order to compute the
            divergence across a SCPS boundary, you add up the divergence at
            each point inside the enclosed region <m>R</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            The proof of this result is simple and is given in the book.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </note>

  <example xml:id="example-ch05-04-ex-26-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Using Green's theorem, compute the integral that represent the flux
        <md>
          \oint_{C}2x-y d y-x^4 d x
        </md>,
        where <m>C</m> is the unit circle (oriented counter-clockwise).
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        From previous material
        <m>\text{Flux} = \int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{N} d s</m>. According
        to the above
        <md>
          \int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{N} d s=\oint_{C} f d y -g d x, \  \text{for }\vec F = \langle f, g \rangle
        </md>
        This implies that in this case the vector field is
        <m>\vec F = \langle 2x-y, x^4 \rangle</m> whose
        <m>\operatorname{Div} \vec F = f_x + g_y = 2 + 0 = 2</m>.
        Additionally, from the theorem
        <md>
          \int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{N} d s= \iint_R \operatorname{Div} \vec F \ dA
        </md>.
        Notice that both region that of integration as well as the
        function behave well in polar coordinates. In this coordinate
        system the region is expressed as <m>0\leq r \leq 1</m> and
        <m>0\leq \theta \leq 2 \pi</m>, while
        <m>\operatorname{Div} \vec F (r,\theta ) = 2</m>. Hence
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\iint_R \operatorname{Div} \vec F \ dA = \iint_R 2\ dA \overset{\text{Polar}}{=} \int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^1 2 r\ dr d\theta = \int_0^{2\pi} \left. r^2 \right |_0^1 d\theta =</mrow>
          <mrow>\int_0^{2\pi } 1 \ d\theta = \left. \theta\right |_0^{2\pi} = 2\pi</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="example-ch05-04-ex-26-3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute the flux of a fluid whose velocity field is given by
        <m>\vec{F}= \left\langle xy, x^2\right\rangle</m>, across the
        boundary of the region in the first quadrant bounded by the circles
        <m>r=1, r=2</m>, the line <m>y=x</m>, and the <m>x</m>-axis
        (oriented counter-clockwise).
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch05-04-ex-26-3">
        <caption>A region in the first quadrant bounded below by the x-axis and above by the line y equals x, lying between the circular arcs of radius 1 and radius 2.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to Example 26.3. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch05-04-ex-26-3" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/Example-26.3.png">
              <shortdescription>A region in the first quadrant bounded below by the x-axis and above by the line y equals x, lying between the circular arcs of radius 1 and radius 2.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>A region in the first quadrant bounded below by the <m>x</m>-axis and above by the line <m>y=x</m>, lying between the circular arcs of radius 1 and radius 2.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Flux <m>= \int_C \vec F \cdot \vec N \ ds</m>. Using the previous
        theorem, the integral can be expressed as
        <m>\iint_R \operatorname{Div}\vec F \ d A</m>. For the vector field
        one has <m>f(x,y) = xy</m> and <m>g(x,y) =x^2</m>. The
        <m>\operatorname{Div}\vec F</m> of this vector field equals
        <m>f_x+g_y= y+0 =y</m>. Now in order to use Polar Coordinates one
        must rewrite the <m>\operatorname{Div}\vec F</m> and the equation
        <m>y=x</m>. Utilizing the equations <m>x=r\cos \theta</m> and
        <m>y= r\sin\theta</m>, one sees that
        <m>\operatorname{Div}\vec F (r, \theta )= r\sin\theta</m> and that
        <m>y=x</m> corresponds to <m>\theta = \frac{\pi}{4}</m> or
        <m>\frac{5\pi}{4}</m>, but the latter is not in the first quadrant.
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\iint_R \operatorname{Div}\vec F \ d A = \int_0^{\frac{\pi}{4}}\int_1^2 \operatorname{Div}\vec F (r, \theta ) r\ dr d\theta= \int_0^{\frac{\pi}{4}}\int_1^2 r \sin \theta \ r\ dr d\theta  =\int_0^{\frac{\pi}{4}}\int_1^2 r^2 \sin \theta \ dr d\theta =</mrow>
          <mrow>\int_0^{\frac{\pi}{4}} \left . \frac{r^3}{3} \sin \theta \right |_1^2   d\theta  = \int_0^{\frac{\pi}{4}} \frac{7}{3}\sin\theta \ d\theta = \left  . -\frac{7}{3}\cos\theta \right |_0^{\frac{\pi}{4}} = \frac{7}{3}\left ( - \frac{\sqrt 2}{2} +1 \right)</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <title>Stream Functions</title>

    <p>
      As we saw in the previous section, given a vector field
      <m>\vec{F}=\langle f, g\rangle</m> defined over an open, connected,
      simply connected region <m>R, \vec{F}</m> is conservative if and only
      if it is irrotational. That is, <m>\vec{F}</m> being irrotational
      implies that <m>\vec{F}=\nabla \varphi</m>, for some potential
      function <m>\varphi</m>. Is there a similar function for source-free
      vector fields? Suppose that, on some open region <m>R</m>, we have a
      differentiable vector field <m>\vec{F}= \langle f, g\rangle</m> and
      that there exists a twice continuously-differentiable scalar-valued
      function <m>\psi</m> (called a stream function) such that
      <md>
        \frac{\partial \psi}{\partial y}=f, \quad \frac{\partial \psi}{\partial x}=-g
      </md>.
      Then
      <md>
        \operatorname{Div} \vec{F}  =f_{x}+g_{y} =\psi_{y x}-\psi_{x y} =0 \quad(\text { since the mixed partials are equal })
      </md>.
      Hence, if <m>\vec{F}</m> has a stream function <m>\psi</m>, then its
      divergence at each point is zero. (Just as if a vector field has a
      potential function, its curl at each point is zero).
    </p>
  </note>

  <remark>
    <title>Source Free and Stream Functions</title>

    <p>
      Let <m>R</m> be an open, connected, simply connected region. Then
      <m>\vec{F}</m> is sourcefree on <m>R</m> if and only if it has a
      stream function on <m>R</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch05-04-ex-26-4">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the function <m>\psi=x^3 y+y^2</m>. Since
        <m>\psi_{y}=x^3+2y</m> and <m>\psi_{x}=3x^2y</m>, we have that the
        vector field <m>\vec{F}=\left\langle x^3+2y, -3x^2y\right\rangle</m>
        is source-free in <m>\mathbb{R}^{2}</m>. Hence,
        <md>
          \oint_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{N} d s=0
        </md>
        for all SCPS curves <m>C</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Moreover, if the curve <m>C</m> is not closed (has starting point
        <m>A</m> and ending point <m>B</m> ), we have that
        <md>
          \int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{N} d s=\psi(B)-\psi(A)
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <title>Summary</title>

    <p>
      On an open, connected, simply connected region <m>R</m> :
    </p>

    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
      <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
        <cell>Irrotational Field <m>\vec{F}=\langle f, g\rangle</m></cell>
        <cell>Source-Free Field <m>\vec{F}=\langle f, g\rangle</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>curl <m>\vec{F}=g_{x}-f_{y}=0</m></cell>
        <cell>Div <m>\vec{F}=f_{x}+g_{y}=0</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Have <m>\varphi</m> with <m>\nabla \varphi=\vec{F}</m></cell>
        <cell>Have <m>\psi</m> with <m>\psi_{y}=f, \psi_{x}=-g</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>\oint_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s=0</m> on all SCPS curves <m>C</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\oint_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{N} d s=0</m> on all SCPS curves <m>C</m></cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{T} d s=\varphi(B)-\varphi(A)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\int_{C} \vec{F} \cdot \vec{N} d s=\psi(B)-\psi(A)</m></cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </remark>

</section>
